  "summary": "The previous analysis correctly identifies that failed items get permanently stuck in `_auto_plan_queued`, but has several gaps: (1) It claims the `break` on line 3490 prevents other items from processing — this is misleading. The `continue` on line 3484 does skip queued items and tries the next candidate within the same cycle, so items don't block each other immediately. The real failure mode is that ALL items gradually accumulate in the queue over time (each fails once and stays stuck forever), eventually exhausting the candidate pool entirely. (2) Simply moving `discard()` into the `finally` block creates a tight retry loop for persistently failing items (e.g., corrupted JSON) — the item fails, gets discarded, next cycle it's re-added and fails again, burning API calls and log space. There should be a failure counter or cooldown per-slug so items that fail repeatedly are skipped with increasing delays rather than retried immediately every 30 seconds. (3) The analysis misses that `_run_plan_async()` (line ~3690) has the identical bug — it also only calls `_on_plan_done` on success, not on exceptions, so plan-inbox and reviewing-plan items can get stuck too. (4) It also misses the lock-acquisition failure path in `_run_ideating_async` (lines 3781-3794): if the lock can't be acquired, the slug was already added to `_auto_plan_queued` by `_auto_plan_check` (line 3487) but is never removed, permanently blocking that item. (5) The rate-limit backoff mechanism at line 3430 is shared between plan and impl via a single `_rate_limit_until` timestamp — a rate limit hit during ideation also blocks auto-implement for 5 minutes, which is an unintended coupling the analysis doesn't mention."
